
---
import "../../styles/global.css";
import Header from "../../components/Header.astro";
import Footer from "../../components/Footer.astro";
import NextProject from "../../components/NextProject.astro";
---

<html lang="en">
  <head>
    <meta charset="utf-8" />

    <meta
      name="viewport"
      content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1.0"
    />

    <title>
      Technofossil Archive — Go Izumita / 泉田剛
    </title>

    <meta
      name="description"
      content="Technofossil Archive is a research and design project that reimagines discarded artifacts and architectural materials as technofossils of the future."
    />

    <meta
      property="og:title"
      content="Technofossil Archive — Go Izumita / 泉田剛"
    />

    <meta
      property="og:description"
      content="Research, Sculpture and Installation Project"
    />

    <meta
      property="og:type"
      content="website"
    />

    <meta
      property="og:url"
      content="https://goizumita.com/projects/technofossilarchive"
    />

    <link rel="icon" href="/favicon.ico" />

    <link
      rel="icon"
      type="image/png"
      sizes="48x48"
      href="/favicon.png"
    />
  </head>

  <body>
    <Header />

    <main>
      <h1>
        Technofossil Archive
      </h1>

      <p>
        Technofossil Archive is a research and design project
        that reimagines discarded artifacts and architectural
        materials as "technofossils" — industrial fossils
        that may be excavated in the distant future.
      </p>

      <p>
        The project draws on Hiroto Ikebe's field research
        in Hallstatt, Austria, and Go Izumita's practice
        of architectural disassembly and reconstruction.
        Through the collection, transformation, and preservation
        of discarded materials, it questions how we perceive
        the value of artifacts and their relationship to time.
      </p>

      <p>
        At the core of the project is the act of "wrapping."
        Through flocking, a process in which short fibers
        are applied to a surface, objects are given a new skin
        while retaining their original forms, allowing
        preservation and transformation to occur simultaneously.
        This process echoes the way geological strata envelop
        and preserve matter over time.
        Together with photographs and research maps,
        the exhibition space was conceived as an excavation
        site from the future.
      </p>

      <p>
        Members / Credits
      </p>

      <p>
        Hiroto Ikebe | Creative Direction, CMF Design,
        Sculptural Fabrication, Research
        <br />
        Go Izumita | Exhibition Design, Display Fixture
        Fabrication, Sculptural Fabrication, Research
        <br />
        Asuka Yazawa | Photographic Works, Cyanotype,
        Project Documentation Photography
        <br />
        Leo Arimoto | Art Direction,
        Graphic and Editorial Design
      </p>

      <p>
        Shinei Plastic | Flocking
      </p>

      <p>
        Exhibition
      </p>

      <p>
        alter. 2026, Tokyo<br />
        October 29 – November 1, 2026<br />
        Nihonbashi Mitsui Hall, Tokyo
      </p>

      <p>
        Photo by Asuka Yazawa
      </p>

      {
        Array.from({ length: 58 }, (_, i) => i + 1).map((num) => (
          <img
            src={`/images/TFA-${num}.jpg`}
            alt={`Technofossil Archive — Photo ${num}`}
            loading={num === 1 ? "eager" : "lazy"}
            decoding="async"
          />
        ))
      }

      <NextProject />
    </main>

    <Footer />
  </body>
</html>
